"R/P FLIP (Floating Instrument Platform) - Ocean Research Platform | DEEP (https://www.deep.com/flip)\nciteturn8view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.deep.com/flip\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Vanguard L1: \nL2: ## Vanguard\nL3: \nL4: Our pilot subsea habitat\nL5: \nL6: Sustained access for ocean science, conservation, and training.\nL7: \nL8: cite1†›Overview cite2†›Status cite3†›Design cite4†›Features L9: \nL10: cite5†Image†cdn.prod.website-files.com L11: \nL12: cite6†sentinel L13: \nL14: ## Sentinel\nL15: \nL16: Our flagship habitat\nL17: \nL18: A large, modular habitat system expanding human access to the ocean.\nL19: \nL20: cite7†›Overview cite8†›Design cite9†›Features L21: \nL22: cite10†Image†cdn.prod.website-files.com L23: \nL24: cite11†Flip L25: ## FLIP\nL26: \nL27: Floating Instrument Platform\nL28: \nL29: A stable research platform for precise data collection in the open ocean.\nL30: \nL31: cite12†›FLIP's future cite13†›History of FLIP L32: \nL33: cite14†Image†cdn.prod.website-files.com L34: \nL35: cite15†Training cite16†Facilities L36: \nL37: cite17†About Us cite18†Newsroom cite19†Get in touch L38: \nL39: cite0†Vanguard cite6†Sentinel cite11†Flip cite15†Training cite16†Facilities L40: \nL41: cite17†About Us cite18†Newsroom L42: \nL43: ‹\nL44: \nL45: Vanguard\nL46: \nL47: Pilot subsea habitat\nL48: \nL49: cite20†Image†cdn.prod.website-files.com L50: cite21†Overview cite21†benefits cite21†Design cite21†Deployment cite21†Use Cases cite21†Missions L51: \nL52: ‹\nL53: \nL54: Sentinel\nL55: \nL56: Flagship habitat system\nL57: \nL58: cite22†Image†cdn.prod.website-files.com L59: \nL60: cite21†Overview cite21†benefits cite21†Design cite21†Deployment cite21†Use Cases cite21†Missions L61: \nL62: ‹\nL63: \nL64: Sector solutions\nL65: \nL66: Accordion Header text\nL67: \nL68: cite21†Text Link cite21†Text Link cite21†Text Link L69: \nL70: Accordion Header text\nL71: \nL72: cite21†Text Link cite21†Text Link cite21†Text Link L73: \nL74: Accordion Header text\nL75: \nL76: cite21†Text Link cite21†Text Link cite21†Text Link L77: \nL78: Accordion Header text\nL79: cite21†Text Link cite21†Text Link cite21†Text Link L80: \nL81: ‹\nL82: \nL83: About us\nL84: \nL85: Accordion Header text\nL86: \nL87: cite21†Text Link cite21†Text Link cite21†Text Link L88: \nL89: Accordion Header text\nL90: \nL91: cite21†Text Link cite21†Text Link cite21†Text Link L92: \nL93: Accordion Header text\nL94: \nL95: cite21†Text Link cite21†Text Link cite21†Text Link L96: \nL97: Accordion Header text\nL98: \nL99: cite21†Text Link cite21†Text Link cite21†Text Link L100: \nL101: cite21†Vanguard L102: \nL103: cite12†FLIP's Future cite13†History of FLIP L104: ### We want to hear from you\nL105: ## The future of FLIP\nL106: After an extensive refit and modernization process, FLIP will be relaunched with new instruments, sensors, computing capability and communication systems. The platform will also be adapted to enable the integration and operation of other technologies that will provide a unique and comprehensive capability set. DEEP’s modular equipment integration system will allow the broadest possible scope of third-party systems onboard.\nL107: Utilizing this system, users will be able to operate and monitor their own propriety equipment in a workspace customized to their tasking. Alongside this, FLIP’s upgrades will put her at the forefront of modern standards in safety, comfort, and ease of operation.\nL108: We’re now asking the academic community to register potential future interest in FLIP. If you’re interested in using FLIP or learning more about how the platform could support your work, we want to hear from you.\nL109: Fill in the form and you will be contacted by a member of the team to discuss your ideas.\nL110: ## Let's get started\nL111: \nL112: cite23†Image: RP FLIP vessel floating on the ocean surface, connected to a large offshore installation with industrial equipment and safety railings.†cdn.prod.website-files.com L113: \nL114: cite24†Image: RP FLIP vessel - Rebuild - Vessel partially submerged in calm ocean water, featuring multiple decks, an observation platform with railing, a radar antenna on top, a waving black flag with a blue circle, and two orange underwater vehicles attached to its lower section.†cdn.prod.website-files.com L115: cite25†Image: RP FLIP vessel - A long, flat vessel floating on calm water under a partly cloudy sky with a small superstructure at one end and a yellow section at the other.†cdn.prod.website-files.com L116: ## The history of FLIP\nL117: FLIP has participated in over 300 research missions around the world, mostly in the Pacific Basin, and has contributed to the advancement of knowledge in many fields of oceanography, such as acoustics, optics, biology, chemistry, geology, and meteorology.\nL118: FLIP, which stands for ‘Floating Instrument Platform’, is an ocean research vessel known for its unique design and ability to ‘flip’ from a horizontal position to a vertical one. FLIP was built in 1962 with funding from the U.S. Navy’s Office of Naval Research and was operated by Scripps Institution of Oceanography.\nL119: The 108 meter (355 feet) long vessel’s ability to change orientation gives it stability in even the stormiest seas, making it possible to study oceanographic phenomena like sound propagation, wave dynamics, and marine life in all conditions.\nL120: FLIP is towed to the desired location and once there its ballast tanks are flooded, causing it to flip from horizontal to vertical in about 20 minutes. In the vertical position, FLIP is stable and quiet, with 90 meters (300 feet) submerged below the surface. This minimizes the effects of wind and waves on the instruments and experiments.\nL121: All images sourced from Marine Physical Laboratory - Scripps Institution of Oceanography\nL122: \nL123: cite26†Image: RP FLIP vessel - Floating on the sea, with visible support frames and a crane on the ship’s deck.†cdn.prod.website-files.com L124: \nL125: cite27†Image: RP FLIP vessel partially submerged in calm open water under a cloudy sky.†cdn.prod.website-files.com L126: cite28†Image: RP FLIP vessel submerged - Black and white photo of a large offshore platform structure rising above the sea surface, with multiple decks, staircases, and an angled radar or antenna mast extending upwards.†cdn.prod.website-files.com L127: \nL128: FLIP in horizontal position\nL129: \nL130: FLIP transitioning\nL131: \nL132: FLIP in vertical position\nL133: \nL134: cite29†Inside the Strangest Ship Ever Built†www.youtube.com L135: cite30†Image: RP FLIP vessel - A black timeline from 1960 to 2024 showcasing key events and images of the research vessel FLIP and related milestones.†cdn.prod.website-files.com L136: \nL137: cite31†Image: RP FLIP vessel - New emblem logo - A black and white technical line drawing of a subsea structure extending above and below the water surface, enclosed in a circular border.†cdn.prod.website-files.com L138: cite32†Image: RP FLIP vessel - Round emblem featuring a schematic side view of the R/P FLIP vessel partially submerged, with the text \"University of California, San Diego\" around the top edge and \"Scripps Physical Laboratory\" along the bottom edge.†cdn.prod.website-files.com L139: In addition to being a platform for a wide array of sensors, FLIP can also operate as a hub for research assets or for scientists to operate safely from, over sustained periods of time. Additionally, it will be able to operate as a platform for technology development and trials in fields such as sustainable energy, material science, and communication.\nL140: You can find out more in the cite33†press release .\nL141: And you can follow FLIP’s refurbishment journey on our social channels @deepengineered.\nL142: cite34†Image†cdn.prod.website-files.com L143: \nL144: cite35†Home cite17†About us L145: \nL146: cite36†Products cite0†Vanguard cite6†Sentinel cite11†FLIP cite15†Training L147: \nL148: cite37†Manufacturing L149: \nL150: cite21†About cite21†Newsroom cite19†Contact Us L151: \nL152: © DEEP. Registered Office: Deep Campus, A48, Tidenham, Chepstow, United Kingdom, NP16 7LQ.\nL153: Registered in England No: 15105319\nL154: \nL155: The DEEP logotype and Engineering Wonder taglines are registered trademarks.Making Humans Aquatic is a trademark\nL156: cite38†Quality policy†cdn.prod.website-files.com cite39†Privacy & cookies cite40†Accessibility L157: \nL158: Follow us on:\nL159: \nL160: cite41††www.instagram.com cite42††www.youtube.com cite43††www.tiktok.com cite44††www.linkedin.com L161: \nL162: cite45†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEvidence that birds sleep in mid-flight - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4976198/)\nciteturn8view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4976198/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 479\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2: cite1†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Search\nL3: \nL4: cite2†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Log in cite3†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov cite4†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L5: \nL6:   * cite5†Dashboard†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L7:   * cite6†Publications†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L8:   * cite7†Account settings†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L9:   * [Button: Log out ]\nL10: \nL11: Search… [Input: Search NCBI] Search NCBI\nL12: \nL13: Primary site navigation\nL14: \nL15: cite8†Image: Close†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L16: \nL17: Search [Input: Search NCBI]\nL18: \nL19: Logged in as:\nL20:   * cite5†Dashboard†www.n