, Rome 00199, Italy\nL74: \nL75: Find articles by cite17†Giacomo Dell'Omo†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L76: \nL77: ^{4}, cite18†Hans-Peter Lipp†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L78: ### Hans-Peter Lipp\nL79: \nL80: ^{5}Institute of Anatomy, University of Zurich, Zurich 8057, Switzerland\nL81: \nL82: ^{6}Institute of Evolutionary Medicine, University of Zurich, Zurich 8057, Switzerland\nL83: \nL84: ^{7}School of Laboratory Medicine and Medical Sciences, Department of Physiology, Kwazulu-Natal University, Durban 4000, South Africa\nL85: \nL86: Find articles by cite18†Hans-Peter Lipp†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L87: \nL88: ^{5,}^{6,}^{7}, cite19†Martin Wikelski†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L89: ### Martin Wikelski\nL90: \nL91: ^{3}Department of Migration and Immuno-Ecology, Max Planck Institute for Ornithology, Radolfzell 78315, Germany\nL92: \nL93: ^{8}Department of Biology, University of Konstanz, Konstanz 78457, Germany\nL94: \nL95: Find articles by cite19†Martin Wikelski†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L96: \nL97: ^{3,}^{8}, cite20†Alexei L Vyssotski†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L98: ### Alexei L Vyssotski\nL99: \nL100: ^{9}Institute of Neuroinformatics, University of Zurich and Swiss Federal Institute of Technology (ETH), Zurich 8057, Switzerland\nL101: \nL102: Find articles by cite20†Alexei L Vyssotski†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L103: \nL104: ^{9,}^{b}\nL105: \nL106:   * [Button: Author information]\nL107:   * [Button: Article notes]\nL108:   * [Button: Copyright and License information]\nL109: \nL110: ^{1}Avian Sleep Group, Max Planck Institute for Ornithology, Seewiesen 82319, Germany\nL111: \nL112: ^{2}California Academy of Sciences, San Francisco, California 94118, USA\nL113: ^{3}Department of Migration and Immuno-Ecology, Max Planck Institute for Ornithology, Radolfzell 78315, Germany\nL114: \nL115: ^{4}Ornis italica, Rome 00199, Italy\nL116: \nL117: ^{5}Institute of Anatomy, University of Zurich, Zurich 8057, Switzerland\nL118: \nL119: ^{6}Institute of Evolutionary Medicine, University of Zurich, Zurich 8057, Switzerland\nL120: \nL121: ^{7}School of Laboratory Medicine and Medical Sciences, Department of Physiology, Kwazulu-Natal University, Durban 4000, South Africa\nL122: ^{8}Department of Biology, University of Konstanz, Konstanz 78457, Germany\nL123: \nL124: ^{9}Institute of Neuroinformatics, University of Zurich and Swiss Federal Institute of Technology (ETH), Zurich 8057, Switzerland\nL125: \nL126: ^{a}\nL127: \nL128: Email: rattenborg@orn.mpg.de\nL129: \nL130: ^{b}\nL131: \nL132: Email: alexei@vyssotski.ch\nL133: \nL134: ^{*}\nL135: \nL136: These authors contributed equally to this work\nL137: \nL138: Received 2016 Feb 18; Accepted 2016 Jul 6; Collection date 2016.\nL139: \nL140: Copyright © 2016, The Author(s)\nL141: This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article's Creative Commons license, unless indicated otherwise in the credit line; if the material is not included under the Creative Commons license, users will need to obtain permission from the license holder to reproduce the material. To view a copy of this license, visit cite21†http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/†creativecommons.org L142: cite22†PMC Copyright notice L143: \nL144: PMCID: PMC4976198  PMID: cite23†27485308†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L145: ## Abstract\nL146: Many birds fly non-stop for days or longer, but do they sleep in flight and if so, how? It is commonly assumed that flying birds maintain environmental awareness and aerodynamic control by sleeping with only one eye closed and one cerebral hemisphere at a time. However, sleep has never been demonstrated in flying birds.\nL147: Here, using electroencephalogram recordings of great frigatebirds (Fregata minor) flying over the ocean for up to 10 days, we show that they can sleep with either one hemisphere at a time or both hemispheres simultaneously. Also unexpectedly, frigatebirds sleep for only 0.69 h d^{−1} (7.4% of the time spent sleeping on land), indicating that ecological demands for attention usually exceed the attention afforded by sleeping unihemispherically.\nL148: In addition to establishing that birds can sleep in flight, our results challenge the view that they sustain prolonged flights by obtaining normal amounts of sleep on the wing.\nL149: * * *\nL150: \nL151: cite24†Image: Inline graphic†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Whether and how birds sleep during long-distance flights has remained a mystery. Here, Rattenborg and colleagues show for the first time that frigatebirds can sleep during flight, but do so in remarkably small amounts.\nL152: \nL153: * * *\nL154: A diverse array of birds, including swiftscite25†^{1} ,cite26†^{2} ,cite27†^{3} ,cite28†^{4} , sandpiperscite29†^{5} ,cite30†^{6} , songbirdscite31†^{7} ,cite32†^{8} ,cite33†^{9} ,cite34†^{10} and seabirdscite35†^{11} ,cite36†^{12} ,cite37†^{13} , engage in non-stop flights lasting several days, weeks, or longer. Given the adverse effects of sleep loss experienced by most animalscite38†^{14} it is commonly assumed that birds fulfil their daily need for sleep on the wingcite39†^{15} .\nL155: However, the recent discovery that some birds can perform adaptively for several weeks despite greatly reducing the time spent sleepingcite40†^{16} raised the possibility that birds forgo sleep altogether during long flights. Consequently, evidence of prolonged flights is not by default evidence of sleep in flight—neurophysiological recordings of the changes in brain activity that characterize sleep are required to answer this question.\nL156: Furthermore, such recordings are needed to establish the amount, intensity, and types of sleep, and the potential implications that flight-related sleep adaptations have for understanding the functions of sleep. Due to the absence of recordings of brain activity during long flights, it is unknown whether birds sleep on the wingcite39†^{15} .\nL157: On land, birds can switch from sleeping with both hemispheres simultaneously to sleeping with one hemisphere at a time in response to changing ecological demandscite41†^{17} ,cite42†^{18} . During such unihemispheric slow wave sleep (USWS) birds keep the eye connected to the awake hemisphere open and directed toward potential threats. Dolphins also use USWS to monitor their environment and can swim during this statecite43†^{19} .\nL158: Consequently, flying birds might rely on USWS to maintain environmental awareness and aerodynamic control of the wings, while obtaining the sleep needed to sustain attention during wakefulness. We tested this hypothesis in great frigatebirds (Fregata minor).\nL159: As Darwin observed during his voyage to the Galápagos Islandscite44†^{20} , frigatebirds are not known to rest on the water despite spending weeks to months flying over the oceancite36†^{12} ,cite37†^{13} ,cite45†^{21} . Their long wings, poorly webbed feet and reduced feather waterproofing make taking off difficult following more than momentary contact with the water.\nL160: To catch food, great frigatebirds rely on large predatory fish and cetaceans to drive prey, such as flying-fish and -squid, to and above the surfacecite36†^{12} . Although previous studies detected potential feeding episodes (that is, slow flight near the surface) primarily during the daycite36†^{12} ,cite45†^{21} , under favourable conditions feeding also may occur at nightcite46†^{22} , as frigatebirds follow ocean eddies predictive of foraging opportunities during the day and nightcite47†^{23} .\nL161: Consequently, frigatebirds face ecological demands for wakefulness 24/7 while over the ocean.\nL162: By recording the brain activity of frigatebirds flying over the ocean, we demonstrate that they can sleep in flight with one hemisphere at a time or both together. Although frigatebirds engage in both types of sleep on the wing, sleep is more asymmetric in flight than when on land. Frigatebirds sleep mostly while circling in rising air currents and keep the eye connected to the awake hemisphere facing the direction of flight, suggesting that they use unihemispheric sleep to watch where they are going.\nL163: Despite being able to sleep on the wing, when compared with sleep on land flying frigatebirds sleep very little, in shorter bouts, and less deeply, suggesting that frigatebirds face ecological demands for attention that usually cannot be met through sleeping unihemispherically. The ability to sustain cognitive performance on limited sleep challenges the dominant view that large daily amounts of sleep are required to maintain adaptive performance.\nL164: ## Results\nL165: ### Flight behaviour of frigatebirds\nL166: We used a data logging device (Neurologger 2A) designed for recording the electroencephalogram (EEG) of flying homing pigeonscite48†^{24} combined with a three-dimensional (3D) accelerometercite49†^{25} to record brain activity and head movements in breeding female great frigatebirds (cite50†Fig. 1a ) flying over the Pacific Ocean (N=14) and after returning to their nest on Genovesa Island (Galápagos, N=9). For each hemisphere, the EEG was recorded from the hyperpallium, a primary visual area (cite50†Fig. 1b ).\nL167: In addition, the birds' movements and altitude were recorded with GPS data loggers. All birds engaged in one or two trips over the ocean (1.21±0.12, s.e.m.) lasting up to 10 days (5.76±0.67 d, s.e.m.; range, 0.26–10.02 d; see cite51†Supplementary Figs 1–8 ) and spanning up to 3,000 km (1988.45±186.33 km, s.e.m.; range, 128.75–3001.42 km). Most birds completed a roughly clockwise loop over the ocean north-east of the Galápagos Islands (cite50†Fig. 1c ).\nL168: The birds spent less time flapping at night (7.31±0.59%, s.e.m.) than during the day (13.49±0.53%; P=6.0 × 10^{−7}; paired two-tailed Student's t-test; cite51†Supplementary Fig. 9a,c ). The frigatebirds' altitude peaked in the hour before sunset and decreased across the night (cite51†Supplementary Fig. 9b ). On average, the birds' altitude did not differ between the day (137.9±4.7 m, s.e.m.) and night (136.5±3.8 m; P<0.78).\nL169: Periods of potential foraging (flight below 20 m) occurred primarily during the day (cite51†Supplementary Fig. 9c ). During the day and night the birds occasionally ascended (1.45±0.15 ascents per day, s.e.m.) to markedly higher altitudes (905.9±25.4 m for ascents >600 m; maximum range 1013.6–1459.7 m; cite51†Supplementary Figs 9b and 10 ; see also cite51†Supplementary Discussion ).\nL170: As previously reportedcite37†^{13} ,cite45†^{21} ,cite52†^{26} , the typical flight pattern of frigatebirds consists of circular rising on thermals (soaring) followed by straight gliding down (cite50†Fig. 1d , cite51†Supplementary Movie 1 ). These flight modes are reflected in the accelerometry recordings by centripetal acceleration (cite50†Fig. 1e ; Methods).\nL171: Circling and straight flight, as determined from a head-mounted accelerometer in great frigatebirds, were associated with slower and faster airspeeds, respectively (5.40±0.23 versus 8.59±0.21 m s^{−1}, s.e.m., P=2.6 × 10^{−8}, paired two-tailed Student's t-test). Time spent circling increased across the day and decreased across the night (P=2.4 × 10^{−6} and P=1.8 × 10^{−7} for the respective linear trends, cite51†Supplementary Fig. 9c ), likely reflecting diel variation in the availability of thermals.\nL172: The birds circled to the left and to the right in equal amounts (cite51†Supplementary Fig. 11 ) and with equal centripetal acceleration (P=0.27, paired two-tailed Student's t-test).\nL173: #### Figure 1. Measuring the brain state and flight mode of flying frigatebirds.\nL174: \nL175: cite53†Open in a new tab L176: (a) Great frigatebird with a head-mounted data logger for recording the electroencephalogram (EEG) from both cerebral hemispheres and head acceleration in three dimensions. A back-mounted GPS logger recorded position and altitude. Photo: B.V.\nL177: (b) Overhead view of a great frigatebird skull showing (1) the position of the cranial bulge (shaded grey) overlying the hyperpallium of each hemisphere, (2) the position of the epidural electrodes (red dots, EEG; green dot, ground) and (3) the data logger (black rectangle) just posterior to the naso-frontal hinge (arrow). Scale bar is 10 mm. (c) All GPS tracks for individual birds coded with different colours.\nL178: The Galapagos Islands are outlined with black lines and the study site (Genovesa) is marked by a star. Ocean depth (m) is coded with grey scale. (d) High temporal resolution (1 Hz) 10 min flight trajectory recorded with GPS from a frigatebird (see cite51†Supplementary Movie 1 for 3D visualization) showing the circling (soaring) and straight (gliding) flight modes typical of Fregatidaecite37†^{13} (Methods).\nL179: (e) Altitude, ground speed and airspeed (computed from the GPS data in (d)), tangential and centripetal (radial) low-pass filtered acceleration, and the absolute value of total acceleration (measured by an accelerometer) for the flight in (d).\nL180: ### Sleep in flight\nL181: The EEG patterns in flight were similar to those observed on land and in other birds. When gliding during the day, the EEG showed low-amplitude and high-frequency activity typical of alert wakefulness. In addition, frequent high-amplitude signals occurred in conjunction with rapid head movements, likely reflecting visual processing in the hyperpalliumcite54†^{27} during active searching for foraging opportunities.\nL182: The rapid head movements and associated potentials in the EEG gradually disappeared and reappeared within the first hour after sunset and the hour before sunrise, respectively. During flapping flight at night, the EEG showed a pattern indicative of wakefulness in both hemispheres occasionally punctuated by isolated high-amplitude, slow waves (cite55†Fig. 2a ).\nL183: During flight without flapping, the wakefulness pattern usually persisted, but was occasionally replaced by continuous high-amplitude, slow waves (cite55†Fig. 2a ). These slow waves were not correlated with fine head movements detected by the accelerometer, and were absent during much larger movements associated with flapping (cite55†Fig. 2a ). Consequently, this EEG activity reflects slow wave sleep (SWS) rather than movement artifacts.\nL184: During circling flight, the angle of the bill relative to the horizon increased slightly in SWS when compared with wakefulness (P<0.004, paired two-tailed Student's t-test), perhaps as a result of the birds drawing their heads up and into the body during sleep, as observed on land (cite51†Supplementary Fig. 12 ).\nL185: On rare occasions, bouts of SWS were interrupted by brief episodes of apparent rapid eye movement (REM) sleep characterized by EEG activity in both hemispheres similar to alert wakefulness, dropping of the head, and twitching like that frequently observed during REM sleep on land (cite55†Fig. 2a , cite51†Supplementary Fig. 13 ; see also cite51†Supplementary Discussion ).\nL186: The birds typically ascended during SWS (rate of climb, 0.154±0.022 m s^{−1}, s.e.m.) and descended during wakefulness (rate of climb, −0.0046±0.0011, m s^{−1}; P=9.1 × 10^{−6}, paired two-tailed Student's t-test), with SWS occurring at higher altitudes than wakefulness (159.4±5.8 m, s.e.m., and 135.3±4.0 m, respectively; P=0.0017, paired two-tailed Student's t-test; cite51†Supplementary Movies 2 and 3 ).\nL187: #### Figure 2. Unihemispheric and bihemispheric sleep in flight.\nL188: \nL189: cite56†Open in a new tab L190: (a) Recording of head acceleration in three dimensions (sway, surge and heave) and electroencephalogram (EEG) activity from the left (L) and right (R) hemispheres showing the transition from wakefulness to SWS following the cessation of flapping (red bars). Brief episodes of dropping (green bars) occur after this episode of sleep.\nL191: Expanded views (bottom) show wakefulness characterized by low-amplitude, high-frequency EEG activity in both hemispheres, infrequently punctuated by isolated high-amplitude, slow waves (*), and SWS characterized by continuous high-amplitude, slow waves, in this case, primarily in the left hemisphere. The red arrow (top) marks an episode of apparent REM sleep (expanded in cite51†Supplementary Fig. 13 ).\nL192: (b) Recording from the same bird showing an episode of bihemispheric SWS (BSWS) and unihemispheric SWS (USWS), including expanded views of both states. The mean duration of episodes of sleep was shorter than these long episodes used to demonstrate USWS and BSWS in flight. These recordings are from frigatebird 13 (cite51†Supplementary Fig. 7 ).\nL193: ### Asymmetric sleep linked to circling flight\nL194: The interhemispheric asymmetry in EEG slow wave activity (SWA; 0.75–4.5 Hz power) varied during SWS in flight. To quantify the frigatebirds' utilization of USWS, we used an asymmetry index [AI=(left hemisphere SWA–right hemisphere SWA)/(left hemisphere SWA+right hemisphere SWA)] to categorized SWS as bihemispheric (BSWS; −0.3<AI<0.3) or asymmetric (ASWS; −0.3⩾AI⩾0.3), with an absolute AI⩾0.6 indicating USWScite57†^{28} . All types of SWS occurred in flight (cite55†Fig. 2a,b ).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nZipper Merge - MnDOT (https://www.dot.state.mn.us/zippermerge/)\nciteturn8view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.dot.state.mn.us/zippermerge/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 58\n"
